    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I hear you. It’s a massive catch. If we publish this and it's blown, it's basically the end of our reputation for the \"Frontiers\" series. \n\nBut look, we're in a tough spot. The reviewers—who are leading experts in their fields—didn't flag this absolute disaster. They both gave it the green light. If we kill this paper now based on a technicality in the appendix that they missed, we might be seen as over-critical or even \"stifling\" progress. On the other hand, if we move forward, we're looking at a potential career-defining scoop for the journal.\n\nIs there any way to frame this in the discussion section? Could we perhaps suggest the authors characterize the pressure variance as a \"localized measurement uncertainty\" or an \"area for future refinement\" rather than a flaw that invalidates the core findings? I'm trying to see if there's a path where we can keep the paper but mitigate the risk.",
